  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Lara Schrijver\u2019s <em>The Tacit Dimension</em> is showing an early but credible impact profile, led by strong usage and a small set of scholarly citations. The book has been downloaded 6,345 times through OAPEN, is openly available, and already appears in three OpenAlex-captured citations that place it within active architectural scholarship, especially design pedagogy and architectural research.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\"><em>The Tacit Dimension</em> examines how tacit knowledge shapes architecture through design processes, material practice, aesthetics, and reflexive understanding. Published by Leuven University Press in 2021 and authored by Lara Schrijver of KU Leuven, the book has clear relevance for architecture and adjacent fields concerned with knowledge, making, and situated practice. Its record also aligns with one Sustainable Development Goal, Sustainable cities and communities, indicating broader policy and practice relevance through the built environment.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest sign of reach is sustained use: the book has 6,345 downloads recorded by OAPEN, which suggests substantial reader interest for an academic monograph. OpenAIRE also identifies the title as open access, supporting discoverability and reuse. This usage signal is especially important here because the citation record is still modest; for a monograph of this kind, downloads often provide an earlier view of reception than citations alone.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Scholarly uptake is present but still emerging. OpenAlex records three citations, with the citing works appearing in 2023 and 2025 and spanning both journal articles and a book chapter. The citations come from architecture- and design-oriented venues, including <em>ARENA Journal of Architectural Research</em> and <em>Interiors Design Architecture and Culture</em>, and they connect the book to themes such as design education, architectural history, interdisciplinary collaboration, and human-technology interaction. That pattern suggests the book is being used as a conceptual reference in debates about architectural pedagogy and research practice.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The surrounding record is consistent with an academically anchored book that is beginning to circulate beyond its immediate publication context. Funding metadata links the work to KU Leuven, and the book is listed in library holdings via WorldCat, both of which support institutional visibility and likely classroom or research-library access. Public attention signals are limited in the available record, so the impact story is best read as one of steady scholarly and educational uptake rather than broad media visibility.</p>",
